.TH PAM_MFA_KEYRING 8 "2026-10-02" "mfa-keyring" "Linux-PAM Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_mfa_keyring \- unlock the GNOME keyring and ZFS home after smartcard, FIDO2 or password login
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pam_mfa_keyring.so
.RB [ mode=piv | mode=login ]
.RB [ file=\fIpath\fR ]
.RB [ module=\fIpkcs11.so\fR ]
.RB [ only_if=\fIservice\fR,... ]
.RB [ skip_if=\fIservice\fR,... ]
.RB [ zfs_only ]
.RB [ prompt_pin ]
.RB [ nofido2 ]
.RB [ debug ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The login keyring (and optionally an encrypted ZFS home) is protected by a random
256-bit secret.  The secret is stored in a per-user wrap file, sealed separately for each enrolled unlock method
("slot"): a PIV card key, a FIDO2 security key (hmac-secret extension) or the
login password (Argon2id).  Enrol with
.BR mfa-keyring (1).
.PP
In the auth stack the module recovers the secret and sets it as PAM_AUTHTOK, so
.B pam_gnome_keyring
and
.BR pam_zfs_key ,
which must come after it, use it.  Once one instance has set the secret, later
instances in the same stack do nothing.
.PP
In the password stack it re-wraps the password slot when the login password is
changed, using PAM_OLDAUTHTOK and PAM_AUTHTOK as set by
.BR pam_unix (8).
For users whose ZFS home is keyed to the secret it then sets PAM_AUTHTOK to the
secret, so a following
.B pam_zfs_key
keeps the dataset on it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B mode=piv
PAM_AUTHTOK holds the card PIN, as forwarded by
.B pam_sss forward_pass
after smartcard authentication.  The module logs in to the card with it and
unwraps the secret.  If that fails, PAM_AUTHTOK is cleared so that the PIN is
never used as a keyring password.  The PIN is never tried when the token reports
it locked or on its final try, or when its length is outside the token's range.
Only place this mode where PAM_AUTHTOK can only be a PIN.
.TP
.B mode=login
(default) If PAM_AUTHTOK is set, it is the login password and unlocks the
password slot.  If it is not set (for example after passwordless
.BR pam_u2f (8),
.BR pam_zfs_key (8)),
the module asks for a touch on an enrolled FIDO2 security key.
.TP
.BI file= path
Wrap file; %h is the home directory, %u the user name.  May be given up to four
times; the first file that exists is used.  Default:
/var/lib/mfa-keyring/%u/login.wrap, then %h/.local/share/mfa-keyring/login.wrap.
The file is read with the user's filesystem credentials and must be owned by the
user and not group or world writable.
.TP
.B zfs_only
mode=login: only act for users whose wrap file records a ZFS home
(mfa-keyring zfs-enroll).  Used by the instance that runs before pam_zfs_key.
.TP
.BI module= path
PKCS#11 module used to reach the card (default: OpenSC).
.TP
.BI only_if= services
Only act for these comma-separated PAM services.
.TP
.BI skip_if= services
Do nothing for these comma-separated PAM services.
.TP
.B prompt_pin
mode=piv: ask for the PIN if PAM_AUTHTOK is empty.
.TP
.B nofido2
mode=login: never ask for a security key touch.
.TP
.B debug
Log more.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/gdm-smartcard-sssd-mfa-keyring
GDM smartcard stack using mode=piv, installed as an alternative for
/etc/pam.d/gdm-smartcard.
.TP
.I /usr/share/pam-configs/mfa-keyring-early
pam-auth-update profile placed before pam_zfs_key: mode=login zfs_only in auth,
and the password stack hook.
.TP
.I /usr/share/pam-configs/mfa-keyring
pam-auth-update profile placed last in auth: mode=login for gdm-password and
gdm-smartcard, for the GNOME keyring.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mfa-keyring/
Per-user wrap files for homes that are not readable during authentication.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mfa-keyring (1),
.BR pam_gnome_keyring (8),
.BR pam_sss (8),
.BR pam_u2f (8),
.BR pam_zfs_key (8)
